202:       "solutionContentEn": "A: A suitable video analysis calibrates a length scale in the plane of motion, determines the actual frame timing and tracks the same reference point frame by frame in both coordinates. The supplied data give x/t=2.0 m/s and falling distance/t²≈5.00,5.00,4.89 m/s²: horizontal motion is uniform and falling distance approximately quadratic in the same elapsed time. Separate representations show approximately linear x(t) and approximately quadratic falling distance(t). Substituting t=x/(2.0 m/s) gives an approximately parabolic x–y trajectory. Superproportional growth alone would not justify a parabola. The rounded data state no measurement-error bounds.\n\nB: 50 km/h=13.888… m/s and 100 km/h=27.777… m/s. Reaction distances are therefore about 13.9 m and 27.8 m. Adding the supplied braking distances 14 m and 56 m gives total stopping distances about 27.9 m and 83.8 m. At fixed reaction time, doubling speed doubles reaction distance while the supplied braking distance quadruples. Their sum therefore grows by more than a factor of two. Before the stationary obstacle, the second required clear distance is therefore about three times, not merely twice, the first. The two given braking distances are consistent with a quadratic speed dependence under the same constant braking deceleration; they do not prove a universal law for all roads and vehicles. Weather, tyres, gradient and actual reaction time limit transfer. A following gap to a moving lead car would additionally require that vehicle’s motion while braking.\n\n**Partial credit, author candidate codex-v2,24 November2026:** Assess only visible evidence. Equivalent physically correct results, representations, allowed rounding and methods receive equal credit. Equivalent evidence counts wherever it appears in the answer; award each subcriterion at most once. Charge an arithmetic or writing slip only where it originates; consistent subsequent work using the learner's own intermediate value 